Editing NEON Files
******************

<div class=perex>

A configuration file is written by people: comments, blank lines, tabs or spaces, a hexadecimal number, `yes` instead of `true`. A tool that changes one option and writes the file back has to keep all of that. This page shows how to:

- read a file into a syntax tree that keeps every character of it
- change, add, remove and rename items, so that the diff is only your change
- find items and comments in the tree

</div>

The libraries for [PHP |php], [JavaScript |javascript] and [Python |python] edit files the same way, as the [specification |format#Implementations] describes. Choose your language above any example and all examples on the page switch to it. Python writes the names in snake_case, so `setValue()` is `set_value()` there.


Reading a File into the Tree
============================

The examples edit this file `config.neon`:

```neon
# database connection
database:
	host: localhost   # the server
	port: 0x0CEA
	# for development only
	debug: yes

services:
	- App\Model\ArticleFacade

mail:
	smtp: true
```

First, parse it into a syntax tree. Unlike decoding into values, the tree keeps every character of the file, so printing it gives the file back byte for byte:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
use Nette\Neon\Neon;

$text = file_get_contents('config.neon');
$document = Neon::parse($text);
$document->print() === $text; // true
```

```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { parse } from '@nette/neon';

const text = readFileSync('config.neon', 'utf8');
const doc = parse(text);
doc.print() === text; // true
```

```python
from pathlib import Path
from neon import parse

text = Path("config.neon").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
doc = parse(text)
doc.print() == text  # True
```
\--

The edits below change the tree, and you save the result by writing `print()` back to the file.


Changing Values
===============

An item is found by its **path**, the list of keys from the root, the same keys that decoding gives:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->setValue(['database', 'host'], 'db.example.com');
$document->setValue(['database', 'port'], 3307);
$document->setValue(['database', 'debug'], false);
$document->setValue(['mail', 'smtp'], true);
```

```js
doc.setValue(['database', 'host'], 'db.example.com');
doc.setValue(['database', 'port'], 3307);
doc.setValue(['database', 'debug'], false);
doc.setValue(['mail', 'smtp'], true);
```

```python
doc.set_value(["database", "host"], "db.example.com")
doc.set_value(["database", "port"], 3307)
doc.set_value(["database", "debug"], False)
doc.set_value(["mail", "smtp"], True)
```
\--

The file then differs in three lines:

```
- 	host: localhost   # the server
- 	port: 0x0CEA
- 	debug: yes
+ 	host: db.example.com   # the server
+ 	port: 0x0CEB
+ 	debug: no
```

Only the value changes; the spaces and the comment after it stay. The new value is written **in the style of the old one**: the port stays hexadecimal, and `yes` becomes `no`, not `false`.

The last call writes `true` where `true` already is, and the file does not change at all: **writing an equal value changes nothing**. A form that saves all its fields therefore does not rewrite the options nobody touched.


Adding Items
============

When the last key of the path does not exist, `setValue()` adds the item. The method `addItem()` adds an item without a key, an item of a list:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->setValue(['database', 'user'], 'admin');
$document->addItem(['services'], 'App\Model\OrderFacade');
```

```js
doc.setValue(['database', 'user'], 'admin');
doc.addItem(['services'], 'App\\Model\\OrderFacade');
```

```python
doc.set_value(["database", "user"], "admin")
doc.add_item(["services"], r"App\Model\OrderFacade")
```
\--

```
  	debug: yes
+ 	user: admin

  	- App\Model\ArticleFacade
+ 	- App\Model\OrderFacade
```

A new item goes to the end of its parent, indented like its neighbours.

A key in the middle of the path that does not exist is an error, so that a typo does not create a new section. With the option `create` set to false, a missing last key is an error too:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->setValue(['databse', 'user'], 'admin');
// Nette\Neon\Exception: No item 'databse' in the path databse.user

$document->setValue(['database', 'pasword'], 'secret', create: false);
// Nette\Neon\Exception: No item 'pasword' in the path database.pasword, and create is false
```

```js
doc.setValue(['databse', 'user'], 'admin');
// NeonError: No item 'databse' in the path databse.user

doc.setValue(['database', 'pasword'], 'secret', { create: false });
// NeonError: No item 'pasword' in the path database.pasword, and create is false
```

```python
doc.set_value(["databse", "user"], "admin")
# NeonError: No item 'databse' in the path databse.user

doc.set_value(["database", "pasword"], "secret", create=False)
# NeonError: No item 'pasword' in the path database.pasword, and create is false
```
\--

A refused edit changes nothing in the tree, not even half of itself.


Removing Items
==============

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->removeItem(['database', 'debug']);
$document->removeItem(['mail', 'smtp']);
```

```js
doc.removeItem(['database', 'debug']);
doc.removeItem(['mail', 'smtp']);
```

```python
doc.remove_item(["database", "debug"])
doc.remove_item(["mail", "smtp"])
```
\--

```
  	port: 0x0CEA
- 	# for development only
- 	debug: yes

- mail:
- 	smtp: true
+ mail: {}
```

An item takes with it the comment on its line and the comments **directly above** it, because they describe it. A comment separated from the item by a blank line is a heading of what follows, and it stays.

Removing the last item **keeps the collection**: `mail` becomes `{}`, because in a configuration an empty section and no section at all often mean different things. Where they mean the same, pass the option `nullable`, and the key is left without a value:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->removeItem(['mail', 'smtp'], nullable: true);
// mail:
```

```js
doc.removeItem(['mail', 'smtp'], { nullable: true });
// mail:
```

```python
doc.remove_item(["mail", "smtp"], nullable=True)
# mail:
```
\--

The comment `# database connection` stands directly above `database`, so removing `database` removes it as well. When you want to keep such a comment, ask for it to be moved to whatever follows:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
use Nette\Neon\CommentPolicy;

$document->removeItem(['database'], CommentPolicy::MoveToNextToken);
```

```js
import { CommentPolicy } from '@nette/neon';

doc.removeItem(['database'], { comments: CommentPolicy.MoveToNextToken });
```

```python
from neon import CommentPolicy

doc.remove_item(["database"], comments=CommentPolicy.MOVE_TO_NEXT_TOKEN)
```
\--


Renaming Keys
=============

The method `setKey()` renames a key and leaves its value as it is:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$document->setKey(['mail'], 'mailer');
```

```js
doc.setKey(['mail'], 'mailer');
```

```python
doc.set_key(["mail"], "mailer")
```
\--


The Tree
========

The tree is made of nodes, and the nodes of tokens, each with its exact text and with its trivia: the whitespace, line endings and comments around it. The method `findItem()` finds an item by its path:

/--div .[code-tabs]
```php
$item = $document->findItem(['database', 'host']);
$item->getValue();                       // 'localhost'
$item->getTrailingComments()[0]->text;   // '# the server'
$item->value->getFirstToken()->text;     // 'localhost', the text exactly as written
$item->getPosition();                    // line 3, column 2
```

```js
const item = doc.findItem(['database', 'host']);
item.getValue();                         // 'localhost'
item.getTrailingComments()[0].text;      // '# the server'
item.value.getFirstToken().text;         // 'localhost', the text exactly as written
item.getPosition();                      // line 3, column 2
```

```python
item = doc.find_item(["database", "host"])
item.get_value()                         # 'localhost'
item.get_trailing_comments()[0].text     # '# the server'
item.value.get_first_token().text        # 'localhost', the text exactly as written
item.get_position()                      # line 3, column 2
```
\--

There are eight classes of nodes: `DocumentNode`, `BlockArrayNode`, `InlineArrayNode`, `ArrayItemNode`, `EntityNode`, `EntityChainNode`, `StringNode` and `LiteralNode`. A node finds its descendants of a given class with `find()`, its ancestor with `findAncestor()`, and the class `Traverser` walks the tree and can replace or remove nodes on the way. A position is always computed from the current tree, so it stays right after edits.

Each library has runnable examples that go further, for example a form over a configuration file: [PHP |https://github.com/nette/neon/tree/master/examples], [JavaScript |https://github.com/nette/neon-js/tree/master/examples], [Python |https://github.com/nette/neon-py/tree/master/examples].

